Here is a piece of writing on a topic of my choosing.

---

### The Unreasonable Effectiveness of the Useless

There is a quiet, persistent pressure in the modern world to be useful. To have a "personal brand," a "side hustle," a "five-year plan." Every hour must be optimized, every hobby must be monetized, every book read must be a "productivity hack." We have been trained to view our time as a resource to be extracted, our passions as potential revenue streams, and our leisure as a failure of ambition.

But what if the most valuable thing we can do is the most useless?

Consider the act of learning a dead language. There is no practical application for Sumerian in the 21st century. You cannot put it on a resume in a way that a hiring manager will understand. It will not help you build a spreadsheet or negotiate a salary. It is, by any rational economic measure, a complete waste of time. And yet, the mental discipline required to parse a cuneiform tablet, the quiet thrill of connecting with a human mind that has been silent for millennia—these are their own reward. The uselessness is the point. It is a sanctuary from the tyranny of the practical.

Or think of the amateur naturalist. Someone who spends their weekends learning the difference between a Cooper's Hawk and a Sharp-shinned Hawk, who can identify the call of a spring peeper, who knows the Latin name for the common dandelion. This knowledge will not cure cancer or solve the climate crisis. It will not make them a penny. But it cultivates a specific kind of attention—a patient, curious, and humble way of moving through the world. It is a practice of noticing, and in a world designed to distract us, the ability to notice is a radical act.

The useless is not the same as the idle. Idleness is a void; uselessness is a fullness. It is the difference between scrolling aimlessly through a feed and spending an hour trying to master a difficult piece of music on an instrument you will never play professionally. It is the difference between watching television and reading a dense, difficult novel that no one will ever ask you about. The useless is an activity that resists the logic of the market. It is a gift you give to yourself, with no expectation of a return on investment.

There is a deep and subversive freedom in the useless. It is a refusal to be a cog. It is a declaration that your life is not a business, that your worth is not measured by your output, and that some of the most profound human experiences—wonder, awe, the slow accumulation of obscure knowledge, the simple joy of doing something for no reason other than the doing—are entirely beyond the reach of utility.

So, the next time you feel the urge to justify your hobbies, to explain how your Sunday afternoon birdwatching makes you a better employee, or how your love of obscure history makes you a more well-rounded person, try to resist. Let the useless be useless. Let it be a small, quiet rebellion. Let it be a garden where you plant things that have no purpose but to grow. In a world that demands we constantly prove our value, the most valuable thing you can do might just be to waste your time, beautifully. [end of text]


